# Target C — audited §§158–159 pure-tooth support regression and exact train coordinates

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This is the collision-free normalization of the two unique pure-tooth results from PR #392. The general exceptional-face closure/Hall and two-sided train-bracing theorems are already §§156–157 and are not duplicated here.

The two results separate combinatorial support from realized metric geometry:

1. §158 gives an infinite abstract regression family showing that circular support incidence, triangle-free train crossings, an odd-unicyclic convex-thrackle diameter graph, rank `q-1`, an all-ones right kernel, and a positive left kernel do **not** by themselves close the pure-tooth endpoint.
2. §159 restores the omitted metric information: every realized core displacement is an exact signed sum of actual unit train vectors. Every actual diameter pair is separated by at least `ceil(D)` stopped-train cuts, so the exact pure-tooth endpoint carries at least `q ceil(D)` diameter/train incidences.

No theorem-state promotion is attempted.

---

# §158 — support combinatorics alone do not close the pure-tooth endpoint

At the exact pure-tooth endpoint the diameter/train first-variation matrix has the algebraic form

\[
\operatorname{rank}A=q-1,
\qquad A\mathbf1=0,
\qquad \beta^TA=0,
\qquad \beta>0.
\tag{158.1}
\]

Its zero pattern is constrained by circular order: rows correspond to diameter chords in an odd-unicyclic convex thrackle, columns correspond to outer-to-outer stopped-train cuts, and an entry can be nonzero only when the corresponding chords cross.

These properties alone are insufficient.

## 158.1 Infinite exact abstract regression family

For every integer `q>=5`, place `2q` train-end slots cyclically as

\[
0,1,\ldots,2q-1.
\]

Use train chords

\[
(0,2),
\qquad
(2i-1,2i+2)\quad(1\le i\le q-2),
\qquad
(2q-3,2q-1).
\tag{158.2}
\]

Call them `tau_0,...,tau_{q-1}` in this order. Consecutive train chords cross and nonconsecutive ones do not, so the train crossing graph is exactly

\[
\tau_0-\tau_1-\cdots-\tau_{q-1}.
\tag{158.3}
\]

It is simple and triangle-free.

Put loaded vertices `0,1,...,q-1` in convex cyclic order and use diameter support

\[
(0,j)\quad(1\le j\le q-1),
\qquad
(1,q-1).
\tag{158.4}
\]

This has `q` edges on `q` vertices, is connected, and has unique odd cycle

\[
0-1-(q-1)-0.
\]

Every pair of its edges meets in the convex-thrackle sense: star edges share vertex `0`, while `(1,q-1)` crosses every `(0,j)` for `2<=j<=q-2` and shares an endpoint with the two remaining star edges.

Place loaded vertex `i` between train-end slots `2i-1` and `2i` in the alternating pure-tooth order. Direct chord alternation gives a `q x q` support matrix `S_q` with

\[
(S_q)_{i,i}=(S_q)_{i,i+1}=1
\qquad(0\le i\le q-2),
\tag{158.5}
\]

and final-row support

\[
(S_q)_{q-1,j}=1
\iff
j\in\{0,1,q-2,q-1\}.
\tag{158.6}
\]

Every row and column has degree at least two.

## 158.2 Balanced rank-`q-1` matrix on this support

For rows `0,...,q-2`, set

\[
(A_q)_{i,i}=a_i,
\qquad
(A_q)_{i,i+1}=-a_i,
\tag{158.7}
\]

where

\[
a_0=1,
\qquad
a_i=2\quad(1\le i\le q-3),
\qquad
a_{q-2}=3.
\tag{158.8}
\]

In the final row set

\[
(A_q)_{q-1,0}=-1,
\quad
(A_q)_{q-1,1}=-1,
\quad
(A_q)_{q-1,q-2}=-1,
\quad
(A_q)_{q-1,q-1}=3,
\tag{158.9}
\]

and zero elsewhere.

Every displayed support entry is nonzero. Row and column bookkeeping gives

\[
A_q\mathbf1=0,
\qquad
\mathbf1^TA_q=0.
\tag{158.10}
\]

Thus `1` is both a right-kernel vector and a strictly positive left-kernel vector. The leading `(q-1)x(q-1)` minor is upper triangular with determinant

\[
3\,2^{q-3}\ne0,
\tag{158.11}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}A_q=q-1.}
\tag{158.12}
\]

Both kernels are one-dimensional and have exactly the algebraic form allowed by the pure-tooth endpoint.

## 158.3 Regression consequence

For arbitrarily large `q`, this abstract family simultaneously has

- circular chord incidence;
- a triangle-free train crossing graph;
- connected odd-unicyclic convex-thrackle diameter support;
- minimum row and column support degree at least two;
- rank `q-1`;
- `A1=0`; and
- a strictly positive left-kernel vector.

Therefore none of these support/rank properties alone closes the pure-tooth endpoint. A successful argument must use metric information: the actual unit train vectors, actual diameter vectors, exact shear coefficients, near-disk geometry, or another source-level relation.

This is only an abstract support/linear-algebra regression. It is **not** a unit-separated realization or a diameter minimizer.

---

# §159 — exact train-cut coordinates reconstruct every core displacement

Assume the exact pure-tooth endpoint of the audited train-shear normal form:

- one unloaded positive-contact component;
- full attachment set `A=B`;
- `T_triangle=q` and `Q^+=0`;
- exactly `q` outer-to-outer stopped trains; and
- the contact-flex quotient is identified with `R^q` by train angular rates.

C65 is binding when this theorem is applied to actual diameter pairs: it identifies `H` with the full actual diameter graph and excludes contact-isolated diameter endpoints. Contact-isolated points remain outside this core coordinate map.

For each train `tau`, choose one exact one-train shore shear and orient its unit contact vector `u_tau` so the moving shore has infinitesimal translation `Ju_tau`. For core vertices `x,y`, define

\[
\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)=
\begin{cases}
+1,&x\text{ lies on the moving shore and }y\text{ on the fixed shore},\\
-1,&y\text{ lies on the moving shore and }x\text{ on the fixed shore},\\
0,&x,y\text{ lie on the same shore}.
\end{cases}
\tag{159.1}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
p_x-p_y
=\sum_{\tau=1}^{q}\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)u_\tau.
}
\tag{159.2}
\]

This is an exact finite identity in the realized geometry.

## 159.1 Sum of one-train shears equals rotation modulo translation

Let `v^(tau)` be the infinitesimal velocity field of the exact shear for train `tau`. Under the pure-tooth train-angle isomorphism, `v^(tau)` maps to the standard basis vector `e_tau`. Therefore

\[
V:=\sum_{\tau=1}^{q}v^{(\tau)}
\]

has all-ones train-angle vector.

Global infinitesimal rotation `R_x=Jp_x` has the same train-angle vector. The kernel of the train-angle map is exactly translation, so one constant vector `t` satisfies

\[
V_x=Jp_x+t
\tag{159.3}
\]

on every positive-core vertex. Subtracting the equations for `x` and `y`,

\[
J(p_x-p_y)
=\sum_\tau\bigl(v_x^{(\tau)}-v_y^{(\tau)}\bigr).
\tag{159.4}
\]

A single train shear contributes exactly

\[
v_x^{(\tau)}-v_y^{(\tau)}
=\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)Ju_\tau.
\tag{159.5}
\]

Substitution and application of `-J` give (159.2).

This formulation is path-independent: `epsilon_xy(tau)` is a signed **shore-separation indicator**, not the number of intersections of an arbitrarily chosen contact path with the train. Reversing the chosen shore orientation changes both `epsilon` and `u_tau`, leaving their product invariant.

## 159.2 Exact diameter/train incidence floor

Since every `u_tau` is an actual unit-contact vector,

\[
|p_x-p_y|
\le
\#\{\tau:\tau\text{ separates }x,y\}.
\tag{159.6}
\]

For an actual diameter pair `h=xy`, C65 puts both endpoints in this core calculation and `|p_x-p_y|=D`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\deg_T(h)
:=\#\{\tau:\tau\text{ separates the endpoints of }h\}
\ge\lceil D\rceil.
}
\tag{159.7}
\]

In the exact pure-tooth endpoint, the canonical ledgers give

\[
e_{BB}-k=0,
\qquad k=d_H+s_Z.
\]

Nonnegativity forces `d_H=s_Z=0`, so `h=q`. Summing (159.7) over all actual diameter edges gives

\[
\boxed{
I_{H,T}
:=\#\{(h,\tau):h\text{ crosses the cut of }\tau\}
\ge q\lceil D\rceil.
}
\tag{159.8}
\]

Thus the realized diameter/train incidence matrix is macroscopically dense; the sparse abstract support family of §158 cannot model the metric endpoint.

For a diameter vector `r_h=p_x-p_y` and unit direction `n_h=r_h/D`, (159.2) also yields

\[
\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}
\epsilon_{h\tau}(n_h\cdot u_\tau)=D,
\tag{159.9}
\]

and

\[
\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}
\epsilon_{h\tau}(n_h\cdot Ju_\tau)=0.
\tag{159.10}
\]

Equation (159.10) is the row-sum-zero first-variation relation; (159.9) is the additional longitudinal metric information absent from support-only models.

## 159.3 Scope and frontier effect

- The coordinate identity is on the nonisolated positive-contact core.
- Contact isolates remain in the full source but outside the train-coordinate map.
- C65 is required before applying the incidence theorem to the full actual diameter graph.
- Repeated contact-path crossings cannot inflate (159.7), because the coefficient is a shore indicator.
- Every train vector has norm exactly one.
- No response coefficient floor or support-only matrix assumption enters the metric identity.

The incidence bound is not itself a contradiction: `q=Theta(D)` and different train vectors can cancel. A closing theorem must combine the dense incidence with the actual odd-unicyclic diameter thrackle, train crossing order, directional geometry, or a simultaneous source-preserving diameter-decreasing motion.

**Disposition:** §§158–159 are audited-working proof-only results. C-II and Level I remain open.
